{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1652 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "19d8ef7e0e20cf74498555818090e769d5f3437f5b5a4767e12e215508b41dc7" :edb/db-before-t 1651 :edb/db-after-t 1652 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534964 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:51:19.270Z" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186221434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445925 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186221435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445926 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186222044 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186222044 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 false] [17592186222044 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+L\\{2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186222044 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 false] [17592186304946 1071 "Consider a function $d(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{d(t)\\}=D(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186304946 1071 "Consider a function $d(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{d(t)}=D(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{-9t}d(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-9t}d(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 false] [17592186304946 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186304946 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-9t}d(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 false] [17592186402100 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-9t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186402100 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{-9t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 false] [17592186402100 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-9t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186402100 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{-9t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." 13194139534964 17592186045417 false] [17592186445913 1080 "The Laplace transform is unchanged." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445913 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445913 1083 #uuid "07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445914 1080 "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{7}{(s-5)^{3}}" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445914 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445914 1083 #uuid "5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445915 1080 "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ vertically." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445915 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445915 1083 #uuid "7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445916 1080 "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445916 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445916 1083 #uuid "83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0e38764" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445917 1080 "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ horizontally." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445917 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445917 1083 #uuid "9b26e88d-f446-59c8-b01e-ca9d9e592906" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445918 1080 "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445918 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445918 1083 #uuid "a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445919 1080 "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445919 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445919 1083 #uuid "c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445920 1080 "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445920 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445920 1083 #uuid "eb1cbeb1-e8a7-518d-a6fb-cbc0586e7fc5" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445921 1080 "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445921 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445921 1083 #uuid "f81a2466-a55c-5f7e-b7c2-eccdac0d7ed6" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445922 1080 "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the right." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445922 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445922 1083 #uuid "ff77f603-3667-5d57-9f2f-4b85ac75f514" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445913 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445915 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445917 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445919 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445922 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1077 "selection" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445919 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445923 1079 #uuid "0524c331-6564-52ef-aba2-9b3e8fe558a4" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445914 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445916 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445918 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445920 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445921 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1077 "selection" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445918 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445924 1079 #uuid "cbbbb7ac-d5cd-51d2-b97c-84f0232078de" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445924 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445925 1069 "q-332187" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445925 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}$ for $s>5.$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445925 1072 #uuid "34e7ccd4-a685-5844-a89f-5c57b735778f" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445925 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}\\}+L\\{7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+7\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>5.$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445923 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445926 1069 "q-332062" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445926 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445926 1072 #uuid "b5d66a4d-16c1-5cfd-be68-7c518337e0a7" 13194139534964 17592186045417 true] [17592186445926 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left." 13194139534964 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad" 17592186445913 "answer-5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" 17592186445914 "answer-7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" 17592186445915 "answer-83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0